7db575f2-4155-4d89-9e15-203f0fcca998	f434baf1-92d7-4214-9f0e-c6db6ee52069	62bc6b02-669d-4c49-b5ca-983bed55bd2b	Prompt: في عندي codebase بـ TypeScript وعندي مشكلة — \nالـ API بتاعتي على /api/orders بترجع 500 error \nبس في production بس مش في development.\n\nممكن تساعدني تلاقي السبب؟\nAnswer: To troubleshoot the 500 error that only appears in production, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Locate the route handler**\n   ```bash\n   find /app -type f -name "*.ts" -path "*/routes/*" -exec grep -l "orders" {} \\; 2>/dev/null\n   ```\n   This will show you which TypeScript file defines the `/api/orders` endpoint. Open that file and look for any `throw`, `next(err)`, or unhandled promise rejections.\n\n2. **Check production logs**\n   - If you are using Docker, run `docker logs <container-id>`.\n   - If you are on a cloud platform (e.g., AWS ECS, GCP Cloud Run), view the logs in CloudWatch/Stackdriver.\n   - Look for stack traces that correspond to the request to `/api/orders`. The stack trace will often point to the exact line causing the failure.\n\n3. **Compare environment configuration**\n   - Verify that all environment variables required by the orders route (database connection strings, API keys, feature flags) are set in production.\n   - A missing or malformed variable often causes runtime errors that only surface after the code is compiled.\n   - Example diff command:\n     ```bash\n     diff <(printenv | sort) <(docker exec <container> printenv | sort)\n     ```\n\n4. **Validate the build**\n   - Ensure the TypeScript compilation succeeded without errors in the production build. Check the `dist` folder for the compiled JavaScript of the orders route.\n   - Re‑run the build locally with the same `NODE_ENV=production` flag to see if any compile‑time warnings appear.\n\n5. **Add temporary logging**\n   - Insert `console.error` or a logger call at the start of the orders handler and inside any `try/catch` blocks to capture the exact error message.\n   - Deploy the change to production and trigger the endpoint; the logs should now contain the error details.\n\n6. **Check external dependencies**\n   - If the orders endpoint calls external services (payment gateway, inventory API, etc.), confirm those services are reachable from the production network. Network restrictions or missing credentials can cause 500 errors.\n\n7. **Review error‑handling middleware**\n   - Make sure you have a global error‑handling middleware that logs the error and returns a generic 500 response. Without it, unhandled exceptions may crash the request silently.\n\nBy following these steps you should be able to pinpoint whether the issue is due to missing configuration, a bug in the route implementation, or an external dependency that behaves differently in production.\n	[-0.04083320498466492, -0.0049606068059802055, -0.02913605235517025, -0.042569082230329514, 0.034049924463033676, -0.00982106663286686, 0.003169647417962551, -0.012084385380148888, -0.05074106529355049, -0.007057013921439648, 0.05362529307603836, -0.019241545349359512, 0.02543729729950428, 0.03506474569439888, -0.031940165907144547, 0.020296426489949226, -0.059233516454696655, -0.07573771476745605, -0.059019867330789566, -0.010455329902470112, 0.06126315891742706, -0.01757243275642395, -0.040619559586048126, -0.046815309673547745, -0.022099068388342857, 0.02801440842449665, -0.0035618890542536974, -0.018400313332676888, 0.014661496505141258, -0.17615161836147308, -0.09395108371973038, -0.058165282011032104, 0.008212041109800339, -0.017545726150274277, 0.0791560634970665, 0.00503738597035408, -0.022793419659137726, -0.0030377875082194805, -0.042435552924871445, 0.013900380581617355, 0.012191208079457283, 0.05496058240532875, -0.02316730096936226, -0.03506474569439888, 0.011803974397480488, -0.03915073722600937, -0.047936953604221344, 0.02870875969529152, 0.02268659695982933, -0.04494589939713478, -0.050046712160110474, -0.04721589758992195, 0.035598862916231155, -0.03004405088722706, 0.023073831573