9ca599dd-a95d-457b-947c-8643caa130f8	f434baf1-92d7-4214-9f0e-c6db6ee52069	77435719-9a29-428c-b115-98b53004b5fe	Prompt: "اعمل simulate لـ crash في خطوة 7 \nمن مهمة 12 خطوة وبعدين resume"\nAnswer: Sure! Below is a simple Python example that simulates a 12‑step process, intentionally raises an exception at step 7 to mimic a crash, catches the exception, logs the failure, and then resumes from step 8. You can copy‑paste this into a local Python environment and run it.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\n# Define the total number of steps\nTOTAL_STEPS = 12\n\n# Function that performs a single step\ndef perform_step(step_num):\n    print(f"✅ Step {step_num} completed")\n    time.sleep(0.5)  # Simulate work\n\n# Main loop with crash simulation at step 7\nfor step in range(1, TOTAL_STEPS + 1):\n    try:\n        if step == 7:\n            # Simulate a crash\n            raise RuntimeError("⚠️ Crash simulated at step 7! ⚠️")\n        perform_step(step)\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f"❌ {e}")\n        print("--- Resuming from step 8 ---")\n        # Skip to the next step after the crash\n        continue\n\nprint("✅ All remaining steps completed successfully.")\n```\n\n**What this does**:\n1. Steps 1‑6 run normally.\n2. At step 7, a `RuntimeError` is raised to simulate a crash.\n3. The exception is caught, an error message is printed, and the loop continues.\n4. Steps 8‑12 run normally after the crash.\n\nFeel free to modify the `perform_step` function to include real work or logging as needed. This example keeps the simulation simple while clearly showing the crash and resume behavior you requested.	[-0.12457973510026932, 0.044995956122875214, -0.015360596589744091, -0.005852917209267616, -0.018816731870174408, 0.007541258353739977, -0.023557329550385475, 0.0292116180062294, 0.04441331326961517, -0.014963340014219284, -0.022934960201382637, -0.044704634696245193, -0.007196969352662563, 0.013771569356322289, -0.032442640513181686, -0.0018836593953892589, -0.055139247328